James sought assistance in combining two data tables with multiple identifying columns, one containing real values and the other with inferred values. He provided a snippet of the data structure, highlighting the need to include all rows from the real dataset and any inferred rows where the `Bucket` column did not exist in the first table. I suggested using `anti_join()` to filter out the irrelevant inferred rows and then combined the datasets using `bind_rows()`. I also cautioned about ensuring that `Bucket` labels were unique by recommending a more comprehensive join method if necessary, involving all identifying columns.
